Hi Hackaday,

Solidon3D 0.3.0 has been released. It is a local desktop application for
adapting downloaded 3D-printing models and constructing printable parts. I
built it around a rule that is easy to state and surprisingly hard to keep:
an LLM may suggest operations, but it never calculates geometry and no source
it produces is executed.

Solidon3D stores every model change as a registered operation with a parameter
schema. Menus, shortcuts and the optional chat all call the same API. One chat
proposal is one transaction, so one undo removes it completely. Project files
contain geometry, values and recipes made from registered operations—never
executable code. The application can be used without the chat, an account or
the network.

The less abstract hack is fit calibration. Solidon generates a stepped gauge
for clearance and press fits. Print it, measure the useful steps, and the
stored material profile drives later holes, pins, bearing seats and snap
features. That can be tested with calipers instead of trusting a software
demo.

The current 0.3.0 work also recognises a hole in an imported STL and changes
its diameter on the original axis as another reversible operation. Since the
first 0.1 demo, 220 curated user-visible changes have accumulated around this
stack, including in-viewport sketching, reusable recipe parts and multi-spool
3MF handoff.

Project overview and downloads: https://solidon3d.de/en/
Technical change history: https://solidon3d.de/en/changelog.html

If this is Hackaday material, reply “details.” I will send the operation/file
format notes, a deliberately wrong STL, the calibration model, measured
results and the 0.3.0 build. I am also happy to answer the failure cases—what
the stack refuses to do is part of the design.

